    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electrons were discovered by J.J. Thomson using cathode ray tubes, establishing the presence of negative charges in atoms.",
        "Rutherford's alpha-particle scattering experiment demonstrated that most of the atom's mass and positive charge is concentrated in a tiny nucleus.",
        "Neils Bohr proposed that electrons revolve in discrete shells or energy levels (K, L, M, N) without radiating energy.",
        "Neutrons are neutral particles present in the nucleus of all atoms except hydrogen.",
        "Valency is defined as the combining capacity of an atom, determined by its outer shell electrons."
      ],
      "id": "eai4nf8"
